('ee26e8a8-a7b3-5c08-95fa-07982c22c73b', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 15, 15, 'Why can the path of the electrons be seen in a fine-beam tube?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The electrons excite atoms of the low-pressure gas, which then emit light', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Collisions excite the gas atoms; they emit light as they return to lower energy levels. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The electrons excite atoms of the low-pressure gas, which then emit light).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('21641a20-aca6-5a3b-a483-035ff874b545', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 16, 16, 'Calculate the accelerating p.d. needed to give electrons a speed of 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹, using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1136', 'V', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'V = v²/(2e/m) = (2.0 × 10⁷)² ÷ (2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹) = 1140 V. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: rearranged ½mv² = eV. A1: V = 1140 V.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('9c9b5a65-727b-5d59-a675-f56795565e6e', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 17, 17, 'What did Millikan''s oil-drop experiment measure directly?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The charges on individual oil drops, which were always multiples of e', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Balancing the drops gave their charges, all whole-number multiples of 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (The charges on individual oil drops, which were always multiples of e).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('ad8fa813-fe8b-52eb-ae11-5b18ba59540b', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 18, 18, 'Calculate the specific charge of the proton, e/mₚ, in units of 10⁷ C kg⁻¹. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₚ = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.581', '10⁷ C kg⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'e/mₚ = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 9.58 × 10⁷ C kg⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: e ÷ mₚ. A1: e/m = 9.58 × 10⁷ C kg⁻¹.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('76c27235-b1f4-5262-ac62-1fa6ecaf8c02', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 19, 19, 'Thomson''s value of e/m for cathode-ray particles was about 1800 times that for hydrogen ions. Assuming equal charges, what did this suggest?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The particles are about 1800 times lighter than a hydrogen ion', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'With the same e, a larger e/m means a smaller mass. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (The particles are about 1800 times lighter than a hydrogen ion).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b6a1a85b-76a1-5648-80bf-bffeaabc68ca', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 20, 20, 'Using e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹ for the electron and 9.58 × 10⁷ C kg⁻¹ for the proton, calculate (e/m for the electron) ÷ (e/m for the proton).', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1837', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Ratio = 1.76 × 10¹¹ ÷ 9.58 × 10⁷ = 1840. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: ratio = 1840.', 1, NULL, NULL),
('67795533-a97c-566b-836b-73f42f798716', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 21, 21, 'Which arrangement did Thomson use to find the speed of the cathode-ray particles?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Crossed electric and magnetic fields adjusted until the beam was undeflected, so v = E/B', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Balancing eE = Bev gives v = E/B. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (Crossed electric and magnetic fields adjusted until the beam was undeflected, so v = E/B).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('6476f16d-47e7-5597-8cca-cfa5fa572059', '3fd36cc2-d7e7-4424-b4b5-11a4a8b7ac04', '932c79b4-ef68-44f7-9381-87d5ddebfa17', 'a-level', '25.5', '9702 coursebook 25.5 · Discovering the electron', 22, 22, 'In Thomson''s apparatus a beam is undeflected when E = 1.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ and B = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ T. Calculate the speed of the particles.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3e7', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = E/B = 1.5 × 10⁴ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: v = E/B. A1: v = 3.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹.', 2, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 242" role="img" aria-labelledby="fge0c8c4bc-t fge0c8c4bc-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fge0c8c4bc-t">Thomson''s crossed fields</title><desc id="fge0c8c4bc-d">Crossed electric and magnetic fields: E = 1.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ between the plates and B = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ T; the electron beam passes straight through.</desc><rect x="120" y="42" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="120" y="160" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="108" y="46" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:700">+</text><text x="108" y="164" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:700">−</text><line x1="139.3" y1="68.3" x2="147.3" y2="76.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="139.3" y1="76.3" x2="147.3" y2="68.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="139.3" y1="133.7" x2="147.3" y2="14